WEBVTT

1
00:00:03.400 --> 00:00:06.740
Fasting blood holds insulin at a few tens of picomolar.

2
00:00:07.260 --> 00:00:11.220
That is about one insulin molecule for every trillion molecules of water.

3
00:00:11.920 --> 00:00:15.260
Every pathway in this course rests on measuring something that scarce.

4
00:00:16.120 --> 00:00:17.180
How can anyone do it?

5
00:00:17.800 --> 00:00:22.380
Rosalyn Yalow and Solomon Berson found a way, and it began as an unexpected

6
00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:24.200
by-product of a different experiment.

7
00:00:25.283 --> 00:00:30.003
They were testing whether people with adult-onset diabetes destroy insulin too fast.

8
00:00:30.503 --> 00:00:35.063
So they tagged insulin with radioactive iodine, injected it, and followed the

9
00:00:35.063 --> 00:00:36.783
radioactivity as it left the blood.

10
00:00:37.423 --> 00:00:41.723
In people already treated with insulin, it left more slowly, because something in

11
00:00:41.723 --> 00:00:42.863
their blood was holding it.

12
00:00:43.403 --> 00:00:45.343
They argued that this something was an antibody.

13
00:00:46.203 --> 00:00:50.103
Immunologists of the day doubted that a protein as small as insulin could raise

14
00:00:50.103 --> 00:00:52.783
antibodies. Then they used it as a scale.

15
00:00:53.867 --> 00:00:57.387
Take a tube. Put in a fixed, small amount of antibody.

16
00:00:58.087 --> 00:01:00.927
Add a fixed, small amount of radioactive insulin.

17
00:01:01.707 --> 00:01:03.287
Every tube gets the same amounts.

18
00:01:03.907 --> 00:01:06.847
The label is radioactive, so a counter can find it.

19
00:01:07.387 --> 00:01:11.707
The antibody has only a few binding sites, and the labelled insulin fills them.

20
00:01:12.650 --> 00:01:14.290
Now add the blood sample.

21
00:01:14.890 --> 00:01:19.450
Its own insulin carries no label, so it competes with the labelled insulin for the

22
00:01:19.450 --> 00:01:24.990
same few sites. A little sample insulin displaces a few labelled molecules, and a

23
00:01:24.990 --> 00:01:26.170
lot displaces many.

24
00:01:26.810 --> 00:01:31.230
The more insulin the patient had, the less radioactivity ends up on the antibody.

25
00:01:32.383 --> 00:01:35.103
Now repeat the test with known amounts of insulin.

26
00:01:35.843 --> 00:01:39.303
Each tube holds the same antibody and the same labelled insulin.

27
00:01:39.963 --> 00:01:41.423
Those results build a curve.

28
00:01:42.023 --> 00:01:46.383
Separate the bound label from the free label, count each, and read the patient's

29
00:01:46.383 --> 00:01:47.703
concentration from the curve.

30
00:01:48.283 --> 00:01:50.243
This is the radioimmunoassay.

31
00:01:50.243 --> 00:01:53.163
Its range depends on the antibody and tracer.

32
00:01:54.217 --> 00:01:59.477
An antibody can tell cortisol from corticosterone, two steroids that differ by one

33
00:01:59.477 --> 00:02:03.837
hydroxyl group. So the assay is only as selective as its antibody.

34
00:02:04.697 --> 00:02:06.517
Bioassays measured what a hormone does.

35
00:02:07.057 --> 00:02:08.797
This counts what the antibody recognises.

36
00:02:09.677 --> 00:02:13.797
Keep this rule: more hormone in the sample means less label on the antibody.
